<HelpLayout title="Logging workouts" description="How to start a workout, log sets, use the rest and set timers, run supersets, skip sets and finish a workout in Badger.">
  <h1>Logging workouts</h1>

  <p>This page follows a session from start to finish: starting a workout, logging sets on the training screen, timers and rest, supersets, skipping, and finishing.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="today-screen">Start a workout</h2>
  <p>Start from the <strong>Today</strong> screen:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free workout</strong> - Tap <strong>New workout</strong> and pick your first exercise.</li>
    <li><strong>From a routine</strong> - Tap a day of your active routine. The routine log preview opens; tap <strong>Start</strong>. See <a href="/help/routines/#starting">Start a workout from a routine</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The Today screen also shows:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>This Week</strong> - How many days you've trained this week, plus a warning for any muscle group you haven't trained for a while.</li>
    <li><strong>Weekly volume chips</strong> - Sets logged per muscle category this week. A colored dot marks a group that needs attention. Tap a chip for the muscle balance detail.</li>
    <li><strong>Monthly report</strong> - In the first two weeks of a month: last month's workout count, total volume (with the change from the month before) and exercises you tried for the first time. Tap × to dismiss it.</li>
    <li><strong>Deload banner</strong> and <strong>plateau card</strong> - When those features are on. See <a href="/help/tools/#deload">Deload</a> and <a href="/help/progress/#plateau-detection">Plateau detection</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>One year ago</strong> - If you trained on this date a year back, a line at the bottom says so, for example "One year ago &middot; Entry &#8470; 63 &middot; Push Day". Tap it to open that session. If you didn't train that day, the line doesn't appear.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="training-screen">The training screen</h2>
  <p>Each exercise opens on the training screen, with three tabs: <strong>Track</strong> (logging sets), <strong>History</strong> (your past sessions of this exercise) and <strong>Graph</strong> (its progress chart). Swipe to switch between them.</p>

  <h3>Move between exercises</h3>
  <p>Tap the <strong>All exercises</strong> pill at the bottom of the screen. From the list you can tap an exercise to switch to it, drag to reorder, or tap <strong>Add exercise</strong>.</p>

  <h3>More options (⋮ menu)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Replace exercise</strong> - Swap in a different exercise. Sets already logged stay attached.</li>
    <li><strong>Switch to alternative</strong> or <strong>Plan B - swap exercise</strong> - Swap for this workout only. See <a href="/help/routines/#alternatives">Alternative exercises</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Skip exercise</strong> - Skip every set left on this exercise. See <a href="#missed-vs-skipped">Skip a set</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Superset</strong> - Group exercises. See <a href="#supersets">Supersets</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Change gym</strong> - Switch which gym this workout is at.</li>
    <li><strong>Set machine</strong> - Pick the machine and attachment. <strong>New machine…</strong> and <strong>New attachment…</strong> at the bottom of each picker add one on the spot.</li>
    <li><strong>Set bar</strong> - Pick the <a href="/help/gyms/#bars">bar</a> on a barbell exercise, at a gym that has bars. <strong>New bar…</strong> adds one.</li>
    <li><strong>1RM calculator</strong> and <strong>Plate calculator</strong> - The plate calculator opens with your current weight, and shows only when plate math applies. See <a href="/help/tools/">Tools</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Exercise notes</h3>
  <p>A notes card sits above the set input for an exercise that has notes or instructions. The info icon in the top bar shows or hides it, and tapping the card opens the exercise notes screen. It opens by itself when the exercise has notes of your own. With neither, the info icon opens it directly.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NOTES</strong> - Your own reminders: form cues, seat settings.</li>
    <li><strong>INSTRUCTIONS</strong> - Built-in technique cues.</li>
    <li><strong>GRIP</strong> - The grip you've selected, when the machine uses grips.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>With a machine set, the notes you write are for that machine, so two leg presses can carry different seat heights.</p>
  <p>The notes screen's heading reads <strong>Notes &middot; MachineName</strong>. Notes you wrote for the exercise in general appear in their own block, and the attachment's notes ("use the thick end") appear in the same card. Increment, rest, instructions and muscles apply to the exercise on every machine.</p>

  <h2 id="logging-a-set">Log a set</h2>
  <p>The Track tab shows the input fields at the top and your logged sets below. The fields follow the exercise type: weight and reps for a barbell lift, time for a plank.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter the weight and reps (or time, distance) with the <strong>+/−</strong> buttons, or tap a field and type.</li>
    <li>Tap the <strong>✓</strong> to save the set.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The set appears in the list, and the <a href="#rest-timer">rest timer</a> starts if auto-start is on.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Edit a set:</strong> tap its row to load it into the fields, change it, and tap ✓. Tap the row again to let go without saving.</li>
    <li><strong>Delete a set:</strong> tap its row, tap <strong>Delete</strong> and confirm.</li>
    <li><strong>Repeat a set:</strong> tap the <strong>Repeat last</strong> chip below your sets. It names the set it will copy, such as a weight and reps or a duration.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="plan-reference">See your target while you log</h3>
  <p>On a workout started from a routine, a line above the input fields shows the plan and your last performance, for example <strong>Plan 8-10 &middot; Last 8 @ 80 kg</strong>. It follows you from set to set.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A set you haven't done yet shows its target in the list, such as <strong>80 kg × 8+</strong>, until you complete it.</li>
    <li>A dim <strong>+</strong> or <strong>-10</strong> in the reps box shows an open or ranged target. It disappears once you change the number.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="rack-stepper">How the +/− buttons step</h3>
  <p>The weight buttons move by the exercise's increment. With a machine set, they use the machine's step instead, and a line under the field says so: <em>Step: 10 lbs &middot; from Cybex Leg Press</em>.</p>
  <p>On dumbbells and kettlebells at a gym whose <a href="/help/gyms/#racks">rack</a> you've set up, the buttons walk the rack's weights (5, 10, 15 up to 50, then whatever comes next), and the line reads <strong>Step: rack &middot; My Gym</strong>.</p>
  <p>You can still type any weight. Past either end of the rack, the buttons go back to your usual increment. With no rack set up, a link under the field offers to add one.</p>

  <p id="progressive-overload">A <strong>Suggested: 82.5 kg</strong> line above the fields means you're ready to add weight. See <a href="/help/progression/#progressive-overload">Progressive overload suggestion</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="duration-entry">Typing a duration</h3>
  <p>Anywhere Badger asks for a duration (the Track tab, a routine's planned sets, quick edit, editing a past set), you can type a time:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>15:00</strong> - fifteen minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>900</strong> - a plain number is seconds.</li>
    <li><strong>90:00</strong> - minutes aren't capped.</li>
    <li><strong>1:30:00</strong> - hours, minutes, seconds.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When you leave the field it switches to the short form, so a typed <strong>15</strong> shows as <strong>0:15</strong>. The +/− buttons move in seconds.</p>

  <h3 id="per-side">Weight per side</h3>
  <p>When an exercise or its machine is set to per side, the weight label reads <strong>WEIGHT (LBS / SIDE)</strong>, and your sets read <strong>90 lbs &times; 8 /side</strong> in your history, on the workout summary and in the exercise's log.</p>
  <p>Badger stores the number you typed. Totals, volume, estimated 1RM and records use it as-is. Older sets have no /side mark.</p>
  <p>Set it on a plate-loaded machine under <a href="/help/gyms/#machines">How weight is logged</a>, or on the exercise under <strong>Weight is logged as</strong> for when you use no machine. The machine's setting wins when both have one. Dumbbells and kettlebells don't have this setting, since their weight is always per hand.</p>
  <p>A routine's weight column shows the same label, naming the machine: <strong>WEIGHT (LBS / SIDE &middot; CYBEX LEG PRESS)</strong>.</p>

  <h3 id="context-chips">Machine, bar, grip and accessories</h3>
  <p>A row of chips under the input fields shows the <strong>machine</strong>, its <strong>attachment</strong>, the <strong>grip</strong>, the <strong>bar</strong>, any <strong>accessories</strong>, and the <strong>rest</strong> that starts when you finish the set. Tap a chip to change it. Long-press the machine chip to open that machine's screen.</p>
  <p>Only the chips that apply to the exercise appear:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Grip</strong> appears when the machine has grip types (set them in <strong>More → Machines</strong>). Pick <strong>No grip</strong> to clear it.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/help/gyms/#picking-a-bar">Bar</a></strong> appears on a barbell exercise at a gym with bars. The bar feeds the plate calculator and your warmups, and records are kept per bar.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/help/gyms/#picking-accessories">Accessories</a></strong> appear on barbell and bodyweight exercises at a gym with accessories, and on a machine marked as supporting bands. Pick several at once; the chip shows the count and colors, such as "2 bands". Sets with an accessory attached don't count toward records (see <a href="#records">Personal records</a>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Add a comment to a set</h3>
  <p>Tap the comment icon on a set row, type your comment ("felt strong", "elbow twinge") and tap <strong>Save</strong>. A dot on the icon means the set has a comment. Comments show, read-only, on the workout summary and in History.</p>

  <h3 id="records">Personal records</h3>
  <p>When a working set beats your best for that exercise, a trophy badge appears on the set row, a message pops up at the bottom of the screen, and the phone buzzes if haptics are on.</p>
  <p>Records are kept separately per <a href="/help/gyms/#bar-prs">bar</a>. While an <a href="/help/gyms/#accessories-and-records">accessory</a> is attached to an exercise, every set of it stays out of records; clear the accessories and those sets count again. An accessory never changes a weight, the plate math or the stepper. What counts as a best is explained under <a href="/help/progress/#prs">Personal records</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="set-details">Warmups, RPE and to failure</h2>
  <p>The <strong>Set details</strong> section under the main fields holds the warmup toggle, the RPE and RIR steppers and the <strong>To failure</strong> chip. It starts closed. Tap its header to open or close it; it stays that way for every exercise until you change it. When it's closed, the header lists anything that's on, for example "Warm-up" or "RPE 8".</p>

  <h3 id="set-types">Warmup sets</h3>
  <p>Turn on the <strong>warmup</strong> toggle before you log a set to mark it as a warmup. Warmups show a "W" badge in place of a set number, and are left out of volume, records and progress graphs.</p>
  <p>To have Badger build the warmups for you, tap the <strong>Warm-up calc</strong> chip beside the toggle; see <a href="/help/tools/#warmup-calc">Warm-up calculator</a>. Set your usual style and number of warmup sets in <strong>Settings → Logging</strong>, and change them for one exercise on its edit screen. If a routine already plans warmup sets, those are used as they are.</p>

  <h3 id="rpe-rir">RPE and RIR</h3>
  <p>RPE and RIR record how hard a set felt. Both are optional: leave them blank and everything else works the same. Tap either label in the app for a short version of this section.</p>
  <p><strong>RPE</strong> rates the effort out of 10. The useful range is 6 to 10:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>10</strong> - Max effort. No more reps were possible.</li>
    <li><strong>9</strong> - Could have done 1 more rep.</li>
    <li><strong>8</strong> - Could have done 2 more reps.</li>
    <li><strong>7</strong> - Could have done 3 more. Bar speed still crisp.</li>
    <li><strong>6 and below</strong> - Warmup territory.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>RIR</strong> counts the reps you had left before failure:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>0</strong> - Nothing left. That was failure.</li>
    <li><strong>1</strong> - One rep left in the tank.</li>
    <li><strong>2</strong> - Two left. A common target for working sets.</li>
    <li><strong>3+</strong> - Comfortable, well short of failure.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The two describe the same thing from opposite ends: RPE 8 is RIR 2, RPE 9 is RIR 1, RPE 10 is RIR 0. Pick whichever is easier to answer and stick with it.</p>
  <h4>Which one to use</h4>
  <p>If you're newer to lifting, RIR is usually easier, since it's a question about reps you've just counted. RPE can tell a hard set from an all-out one, where RIR reads 0 for both. Go with your honest read; most people's scale settles after a month or two.</p>
  <p>The fields are hidden while the warmup toggle is on. Logged values show as small badges on each set row, and you can add or change them later by tapping a past set in your history. Logged RIR also feeds the RPE/RIR trigger of <a href="/help/tools/#deload">deload detection</a>, when that trigger is on.</p>

  <h3 id="to-failure">Mark a set to failure</h3>
  <p>Tap the <strong>To failure</strong> chip next to the steppers to record that a set went all the way. It sets RIR to 0, and tapping it again clears it. A set to failure is about effort: if you were aiming for 8 and got 6, log the 6 (see <a href="#missed-vs-skipped">Skip a set, or log a short one</a>).</p>

  <h3 id="amrap">AMRAP sets (8+)</h3>
  <p id="amrap-target">An <strong>8+</strong> in your plan is an AMRAP set: do at least 8, then <em>as many reps as possible</em>. A <strong>1+</strong> set means go to failure. Log the reps you actually got; the set meets the plan once you pass the floor. How to write one into a routine is under <a href="/help/routines/#amrap-targets">Open-topped targets</a>.</p>
  <p>AMRAP is what the plan asks for, and <strong>To failure</strong> is what you record afterward. They're independent.</p>
  <p>Tap To failure on a plain 8-rep set if that's how it went, or stop an 8+ set at 12 with reps left. In a program with a <a href="/help/progression/#rules">progression rule</a>, the open set is often the <a href="/help/progression/#driver-set">driver</a>, so the reps you log on it decide next session.</p>

  <h2 id="rest-timer">Rest timer</h2>
  <p>The rest timer shows as a chip near the top of the training screen. It counts down after you save a set, then plays a sound, vibrates, or both (set this in Settings). It doesn't start after the last working set of your last exercise.</p>
  <p>Tap the chip to open the rest timer sheet:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>While a timer runs:</strong> <strong>Stop</strong>, <strong>+30s</strong>, or <strong>Restart</strong> from the full duration.</li>
    <li><strong>When none is running:</strong> pick a preset or type a number of seconds, then tap <strong>Start</strong> for a one-off timer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How long the rest is</h3>
  <p>Badger uses the first of these that's set:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A one-off duration you set in the rest timer sheet</li>
    <li>The exercise's own rest (on the exercise notes screen)</li>
    <li>The routine's rest for that exercise</li>
    <li>The default in <a href="/help/settings/#rest-timer">Settings → Rest timer</a></li>
    <li>The last duration you used</li>
    <li>90 seconds</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Warmup sets use a separate, shorter rest, set per exercise or in Settings.</p>

  <h2 id="set-timer">Set timer (timed exercises)</h2>
  <p>A timed exercise (a plank, a carry) gets a countdown for the set itself, started from the training screen. It keeps running when you lock the phone or switch apps.</p>

  <h3 id="timer-end">When the timer ends</h3>
  <p>When the countdown reaches zero, Badger fills in the set's duration and starts your rest timer. The set stays open until you tap its check, and that tap doesn't restart the rest.</p>
  <p>To have Badger log the set for you as well, turn on <strong>Settings → Set timer → Log the set when the timer ends</strong>. To start rest yourself, turn off <strong>Start rest when the timer ends</strong>. In a superset, rest follows the superset's own rule. If you stop the timer early, neither happens.</p>

  <h3>What Badger says while the set runs</h3>
  <p>With the app open, Badger counts you in with "3, 2, 1, Go", warns you at 30 and 10 seconds left, and says "Time" at the end. On a long set it also calls out the minutes left: every minute under ten, every five minutes up to thirty, every ten above that.</p>
  <p>Pick the voice in <strong>Settings → Set timer → Set timer voice</strong>: <strong>Female</strong>, <strong>Male</strong> or <strong>Off</strong>. Tap a choice to hear it. The voices are built into the app, so they work offline. If music is playing, it gets quieter while Badger speaks.</p>

  <h3>When the phone is locked</h3>
  <p>On iPhone, the spoken countdown keeps going through a locked screen. On Android, or on an iPhone where a call or another app took the audio, a <strong>Set complete</strong> notification arrives when the set ends instead.</p>
  <p>Voice <strong>Off</strong> doesn't stop that notification. To make it silent, turn off <strong>Settings → Set timer → Set timer sound</strong>; the rest timer has its own sound setting. That toggle only covers the notification - the spoken countdown keeps talking with it off.</p>

  <h2 id="lock-screen">Timers on the lock screen</h2>
  <h3>iPhone: Live Activities</h3>
  <p>On iOS 16.2 and later, both timers appear as a Live Activity: a countdown card on the lock screen, and a compact countdown in the Dynamic Island on models that have one. Tap it to go back to your workout.</p>
  <p>Both cards name the exercise. The rest card reads "Rest done" when it finishes; a timed set shows "SET" while it runs and "SET DONE" at the end.</p>
  <p>The first time, iOS asks "Allow Live Activities from Badger?". Allow it to see the countdown outside the app; you can change it later in iOS Settings under Badger.</p>
  <p>On iOS 26.6 and later, a rest timer that finishes while the phone is locked plays the Badger sound you picked. On earlier versions, iOS plays its standard sound instead.</p>

  <h3 id="workout-notification">Android: workout notification</h3>
  <p>During a workout, a notification shows the exercise you're on and your next set's target, such as "85 kg &times; 8", so you can check it from the lock screen. It adds the elapsed time once the workout timer is running, and tapping it takes you back to that exercise.</p>
  <p>It updates as you switch exercises, move through a superset and complete sets, and reads "No target set" when the exercise has no sets left. During rest it shows the countdown, then goes back to the target.</p>
  <p>Turn it off in <strong>Settings → App → Workout notification</strong>. Workouts logged for a past date don't get one.</p>

  <h2 id="supersets">Supersets</h2>
  <p>A superset is a group of exercises you alternate between. After you log a set of one, Badger switches to the next exercise in the group with no rest in between. After the last one in the round, the rest timer starts and you go back to the first exercise that still has sets left.</p>
  <p>Grouped exercises show as a named, colored block in exercise lists, and the training screen shows the superset's name under the exercise name.</p>

  <h3>Create a superset</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the training screen's <strong>⋮ menu</strong> and tap <strong>Superset…</strong>, or open <strong>All exercises</strong> and tap <strong>Create superset</strong>.</li>
    <li>Check two or more exercises and confirm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>An exercise that's already in another superset moves to the new one (its row says so).</p>

  <h3>Edit a superset</h3>
  <p>Tap the superset's name in the app bar, or its block header in any exercise list. In the editor you can:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Rename it and pick its color.</li>
    <li>Drag exercises into the order you alternate in.</li>
    <li>Remove one exercise. A superset left with one exercise is removed.</li>
    <li>Add exercises.</li>
    <li>Dissolve it, with an Undo. Your logged sets aren't affected.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The same editor works in a routine, and on a past workout from its summary: tap the superset header above an exercise card.</p>

  <h2 id="missed-vs-skipped">Skip a set, or log a short one</h2>
  <p>If you did the set but came up short, log what you did. If you didn't do it at all, skip it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skip one set:</strong> tap its row, then tap <strong>Skip</strong>. Tap <strong>Unskip</strong> to undo.</li>
    <li><strong>Skip the rest of an exercise:</strong> open the <strong>⋮ menu</strong> and tap <strong>Skip exercise</strong>, or long-press the exercise in <strong>All exercises</strong> and tap <strong>Skip remaining sets</strong>. The same menu item reads <strong>Unskip exercise</strong> once every set on it is skipped.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A skipped set stays on the training screen, struck through, and is left out of your history, all calculations, and plateau and deload detection.</p>
  <p>A short set is a real set. Aiming for 8 and getting 6? Log the 6. It counts toward volume, shows in your history and feeds plateau and deload detection. On a routine workout, the summary marks it as under plan.</p>
  <p>Skipping the last set left on an exercise moves you to the next exercise that still has work, the same as completing it. Turn off <strong>Auto-advance to next exercise</strong> in <strong>Settings → Logging</strong> to stay put.</p>

  <h2 id="finishing">Finish a workout</h2>
  <p>On the Today screen, tap <strong>End workout</strong>. The workout summary opens. On a routine workout, it can offer to update the routine with heavier weights you lifted; see <a href="/help/progression/#auto-progress">Auto-progress routine weights</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="resuming">Resume a finished workout</h3>
  <p>Forgot an exercise? Open <strong>today's</strong> workout summary and tap <strong>Resume workout</strong>. It's offered for today's workout only.</p>
  <p>The workout timer picks up where it stopped when you log the first new set, so the time between finishing and resuming isn't counted. Editing or re-marking sets on a finished workout doesn't change its duration.</p>

  <h3 id="exercise-order">Exercises done out of order</h3>
  <p>Badger records the order you actually did your exercises. When you complete the first set of an exercise, it moves into place after the ones you've already done, and exercises you haven't started stay queued after it. Your routine's planned order stays the same. You can still drag to reorder, and a drag sticks.</p>

  <h2 id="progression">Progression rules and suggestions</h2>
  <p>How a routine's rules, training maxes and percentage sets set your weights, and how the progressive overload suggestion and auto-progress work, is on the <a href="/help/progression/">Progression</a> page.</p>
  <ul>
    <li id="rules-in-preview"><a href="/help/progression/#when-a-rule-acts">What the routine log preview shows</a></li>
    <li id="percent-sets-preview"><a href="/help/progression/#percent-sets">Percentage sets</a></li>
    <li id="waved-day-preview"><a href="/help/progression/#waves">Waved days</a></li>
    <li id="driver-sets"><a href="/help/progression/#driver-set">The driver set</a></li>
    <li id="auto-progress"><a href="/help/progression/#auto-progress">Auto-progress routine weights</a></li>
  </ul>
</HelpLayout>
